📌 2. Windows Architecture
🎯 Goal: You do not need to become a Windows kernel developer. You need enough architecture knowledge to
understand processes, privileges, system calls, security boundaries and where endpoint activity comes from.

🔹 2.1 User Mode vs Kernel Mode
- User mode: Applications and many Windows services operate with restricted privileges. Examples include browsers,
  Office applications and many user processes.
- Kernel mode: Core operating-system components, kernel code and drivers operate with much higher privilege.
- SOC relevance: Malware attempting to gain higher privileges, load drivers or interact with protected components can
  create important detection signals.
🔹 2.2 Major Windows Components
- Windows Kernel: Provides core operating-system functionality.
- Executive: Coordinates components such as process/thread management, memory, I/O and security-related functions.
- Services: Background components that can run without an interactive user session.
- Winlogon / authentication components: Participate in user logon and session initialization.
- LSASS: A security-sensitive Windows process involved in authentication and credential-related operations. Unexpected
  access to it is a major investigation signal.
- Windows Defender / security components: Provide endpoint protection and telemetry.
🔹 2.3 Processes, Threads and Handles
- Process: A running program with its own virtual address space and security context.
- Thread: A unit of execution inside a process.
- Handle: A reference used by a process to access operating-system objects such as files, registry keys and processes.🔎 SOC question to ask: Which process started this process, under which account, with what command line, and what
  did it access?
📌 3. Processes & Services
🔹 3.1 Processes — Concepts to Know
- Process name and executable path
- PID (Process ID)
- Parent process / child process relationship
- User account running the process
- Command-line arguments
- Integrity level and privileges
- Network connections associated with a process
- Loaded modules/DLLs at a conceptual level
- Process creation and termination
🔹 3.2 Normal Windows Processes You Should Recognize
Process	Why you should recognize it
explorer.exe	Windows shell / desktop environment.
svchost.exe	Hosts Windows services; many legitimate instances exist.
services.exe	Service Control Manager process.
lsass.exe	Security authority process; highly security-sensitive.
winlogon.exe	Windows logon/session component.
csrss.exe	Client/Server Runtime Subsystem; critical Windows process.
smss.exe	Session Manager; early Windows initialization.
System	Kernel/system activity; investigate carefully rather than assuming maliciousness.
powershell.exe	PowerShell process; legitimate but frequently abused.
cmd.exe	Command Prompt; legitimate but can execute malicious commands.


🔹 3.3 Services
- Understand what a Windows service is and why services can start automatically.
- Know the Service Control Manager (SCM) concept.
- Understand service name vs display name.
- Know that attackers may abuse services for persistence or execution.
- Learn to investigate unusual service creation, executable paths, accounts and start types.
Get-Service
Get-Service | Where-Object {$_.Status -eq 'Running'}
Get-CimInstance Win32_Service | Select Name,State,StartMode,StartName,PathName
🔹 3.4 SOC Investigation Checklist — Process
- Is the executable located in an expected directory?
- Is the filename pretending to be a legitimate process?
- What is the parent process?
- What account executed it?
- What command line was used?
- Did it create network connections?
- Did it create files, scheduled tasks, services or registry changes?
📌 4. Users, Groups & Permissions
🔹 4.1 Identity Basics
- Local user accounts vs domain accounts
- Security groups and group membership
- Administrator vs standard-user context
- Service accounts and why their privileges matter
- Security identifiers (SIDs) at a conceptual level
🔹 4.2 Access Control
- Windows uses access control lists (ACLs) to control access to securable objects.
- ACE: An access-control entry describing an allow/deny permission for a security principal.
- Know common permissions: read, write, modify, execute and full control.
- Understand inheritance conceptually: permissions can flow from parent objects to children.
🔹 4.3 Privileges and UAC
- UAC: User Account Control helps reduce the risk of unauthorized elevation of privileges.
- Understand the difference between being an administrator and running a process with elevated privileges.
- Know the idea of privilege escalation: obtaining permissions beyond the attacker's initial access.
🔹 4.4 Commands for Practice
whoami
whoami /all
whoami /groups
whoami /priv
whoami /user
net user
net localgroup administrators
Get-LocalUser
Get-LocalGroup
Get-LocalGroupMember Administrators
🔎 SOC questions: Who performed the action? What account was used? Was the account privileged? Was privilege
elevation involved?

📌 5. Windows File System
🔹 5.1 NTFS Fundamentals
- Understand files, directories, paths and file extensions.
- Understand NTFS permissions and inheritance.
- Know that file metadata and timestamps can support investigations.
- Understand that a file's extension does not prove what the file actually is.
🔹 5.2 Important Locations for SOC Analysts
Location	SOC relevance
C:\Windows\System32	Core Windows binaries; unexpected modifications or look-alikes deserve investigation.
C:\Windows\Temp	Temporary files; may contain installer artifacts or malicious activity.
%TEMP%	User temporary directory; used by legitimate software and sometimes malware.
%APPDATA%	User application data; sometimes abused for persistence.
%LOCALAPPDATA%	Per-user local application data.
Downloads	Common location for downloaded files and user-delivered payloads.
Startup folders	Potential persistence location.


🔹 5.3 File Investigation Commands
Get-ChildItem C:\Windows\Temp
Get-Item 'C:\path\file.exe' | Format-List *
Get-FileHash 'C:\path\file.exe' -Algorithm SHA256
Get-Acl 'C:\path\file.exe' | Format-List
- Learn to identify file path, size, timestamps, hash, owner and ACLs.
- For suspicious files, compare path + signer + hash + process context rather than judging by filename alone.
📌 6. Windows Registry
🔹 6.1 What the Registry Is
- A hierarchical configuration database used by Windows and applications.
- It contains settings for the operating system, users, applications and security-related configuration.
- Registry data is organized into hives, keys, subkeys and values.
🔹 6.2 Main Hives to Recognize
- HKLM (HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE): Machine-wide configuration.
- HKCU (HKEY_CURRENT_USER): Configuration for the current user.
- HKCR: Combined view related to classes/file associations.
- HKU: User profile information for loaded user accounts.
🔹 6.3 Registry and Persistence
- Learn the concept of Run/RunOnce persistence locations.
- Understand that attackers may modify registry configuration to execute code automatically.
- Do not memorize every registry key; learn the major persistence categories and how to investigate changes.
reg query HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run
reg query HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run
Get-ItemProperty 'HKCU:\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run'
🔎 SOC investigation: identify what value changed, what executable/script it points to, who changed it, when it changed,
and whether the target is legitimate.

📌 7. PowerShell
🔹 7.1 Why PowerShell Is Critical for SOC
- PowerShell is a legitimate Windows automation and administration framework.
- Because it is powerful and widely available, attackers may use it for execution, discovery, downloading, persistence and
  lateral movement.
- The SOC objective is not to block PowerShell blindly; it is to identify suspicious usage and context.
🔹 7.2 PowerShell Fundamentals
- Cmdlets and verbs/nouns
- Objects and pipelines
- Variables
- Scripts (.ps1)
- Execution context and privileges
- Modules
- Encoded commands as an investigation clue
- Remote execution concepts
🔹 7.3 Commands You Should Be Comfortable Reading
Get-Process
Get-Service
Get-ChildItem
Get-ItemProperty
Get-ComputerInfo
Get-NetIPConfiguration
Get-NetTCPConnection
Get-WinEvent -LogName Security -MaxEvents 20
🔹 7.4 Logging Concepts
- PowerShell operational logging
- Script Block Logging
- Module Logging
- Transcription
- Understand that logging configuration affects the evidence available to a SOC analyst.
🔹 7.5 Suspicious PowerShell Indicators
- Unexpected PowerShell launched by an unusual parent process.
- Highly obfuscated or encoded commands.
- Commands that download or execute remote content.
- PowerShell running under an unexpected account or from an unusual application.
- Activity that combines PowerShell with persistence, credential access or lateral movement.⚠️ Important: a suspicious-looking PowerShell command is an investigation lead, not automatically proof of malicious activity.
📌 8. Windows Security
🔹 8.1 Authentication
- Understand authentication vs authorization.
- Know the basic purpose of NTLM and Kerberos.
- Understand logon sessions and authentication events at a conceptual level.
- Learn why repeated failed logons, unusual successful logons and abnormal authentication patterns matter to a SOC.
🔹 8.2 Windows Security Architecture
- Security accounts and security principals
- Access tokens
- Privileges
- UAC
- Security policy
- Protected processes at a conceptual level
🔹 8.3 Windows Defender
- Understand antivirus vs broader endpoint detection and response concepts.
- Learn what detections, alerts, quarantine and remediation mean.
- Understand that Defender telemetry can contribute to an investigation alongside Windows events.
🔹 8.4 Windows Firewall
- Understand inbound vs outbound rules.
- Know profiles conceptually: Domain, Private and Public.
- Understand how firewall activity can support network investigations.
Get-NetFirewallProfile
Get-NetFirewallRule | Select DisplayName,Enabled,Direction,Action
